      <![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><h3>Chips, export controls and the long game</h3><div>The Strategic Brief (newsletter), January 2026</div><div><br/></div><div>The argument in this issue: export controls on advanced chips buy time, not victory. Restricting the newest accelerators slows a rival's frontier training runs, but it also creates a guaranteed domestic market for that rival's own chip designers, who no longer have to compete with the best foreign product at home.</div><div><br/></div><div>Three things to watch over the next few years:</div><ul><li><b>Yield, not node.</b> A rival can announce an advanced process; the question is what fraction of wafers come out usable and at what cost. Low yields make a chip a strategic demonstration rather than an industrial one.</li><li><b>The tooling chokepoint.</b> Lithography and some deposition tools are made by a handful of firms in allied countries. Controls on servicing and spare parts matter as much as controls on new machines.</li><li><b>Talent flows.</b> Engineers moving between firms and countries carry more know-how than any shipment.</li></ul><div><br/></div><div>The newsletter's conclusion is that the policy is best judged in five-year windows. Measured quarter to quarter it always looks either like a decisive win or a total failure, and it is neither.</div></en-note>]]>
    </content>
  </note>
</en-export>
